            <ArticleTitle>Intravitreal injections using a novel conjunctival mould: a comparison with a conventional technique.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>395-7</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1136/bjophthalmol-2012-302155</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="AIMS" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">The aims were to compare a novel conjunctival mould used to assist the delivery of intravitreal drugs to a conventional technique with respect to patient, surgeon and cost benefit.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">A prospective review of 200 intravitreal injections was undertaken, 100 using a 'conventional' freehand technique (group 1) and 100 using a novel conjunctival mould (group 2). Intraoperative visual analogue scale (VAS) pain scores, patient preference, surgeon perception of the ease of insertion of the conjunctival mould were recorded as well as a cost comparison.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">VAS pain score in the conventional group was 2.58 compared to 1.38 in the conjunctival mould group (p&lt;0.01). The surgeon reported the insertion of the conjunctival mould as easy in 89 cases, moderate in 10 cases, and difficult in one case. The cost saving with a conjunctival mould pack compared to a conventional pack was £7.70; an annual saving of £19,250 for the trust.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">The reduction in the VAS pain score with the conjunctival mould was statistically significant (p&lt;0.01). The surgeons found that the device, which was easy to insert, offered excellent globe stability and a safe, reproducible entry site and angle of needle insertion.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
